seg-6e7c5f0e34f3 = Frames and time

# text
# c1: nutation::teme_to_gcrs
# c2: frames::teme_to_ecef
seg-01d1bedb0595 = The engine propagates in TEME (true equator, mean equinox of date), its native frame. Moving objects are rotated to the GCRS by the <c1/> reduction (IAU { "2006" } precession and IAU 2000B nutation, where IAU is the International Astronomical Union), which reproduces Vallado's published TEME-to-GCRF (Geocentric Celestial Reference Frame) example to { "0.11" } m, for CZML and STK, and to the Earth-fixed frame by <c2/> for KML and GeoJSON. The Earth-fixed rotation is the Greenwich mean sidereal angle with UT1 taken equal to UTC and polar motion not applied — the same reduction the SP3 (Standard Product { "3" }) export uses. At a GPS orbit radius the neglected UT1−UTC of up to { "0.9" } s moves a satellite's longitude by up to about { "1.7" } km; ground sites are unaffected, since they are given in latitude and longitude.

# text
# c1: ephemeris
seg-4eaa7b3e6946 = The <c1/> kind is the exception: its engine output already carries GCRS and Earth-fixed positions (with the scenario's UT1−UTC and polar motion, or a real International Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service (IERS) series), and those are written unchanged.

# text
seg-2299da743a4c = Every time is UTC. Leap seconds are not modelled: an offset across a leap second is counted as if every day had { "86 400" } s, as the engine's time grids count.

