# text
# c1: contact-schedule
# c2: acm-link
# c3: passes
seg-d7bd20cb8710 = the <c1/> and <c2/> kinds plan downlink contacts and coding; export a <c3/> scenario for the same satellite and station to see the geometry

# text
# c1: spectrum
seg-f587b4df1635 = SigMF holds complex baseband samples, which only the <c1/> kind synthesises

# text
# c1: aocs
seg-d102fb18ce8d = the <c1/> kind simulates attitude about the centre of mass with no orbit or ground position, so there is nothing to place on the Earth

# text
seg-ecc0e31b0369 = the scenario input carries no horizontal position (no latitude and longitude, no Earth-centred coordinates, no orbital elements beyond at most an altitude), so there is nothing to place on the Earth

# text
# c1: eo-radiometry
# c2: ascent-losses
seg-2e0825b27359 = the <c1/> and <c2/> kinds compute budgets with no Earth position

# text
seg-1f17b963c7a6 = an STK ephemeris describes a vehicle's position and velocity against time; this scene has only fixed points or untimed tracks (a fixed point is an STK Facility, not an ephemeris)

# text
seg-f79d0ce7b945 = no member scenario of the campaign has anything this format describes; export a member on its own to see its reason

# text
# c1: leo-signal
# c2: leo-pass
# c3: leo-pnt-chain
seg-08b7e027318a = the <c1/> kind analyses signal designs (spectra, tracking, acquisition, compatibility) with no satellite or user position; export the <c2/> or <c3/> scenario that flies the design

# text
# c1: leo-pass
# c2: leo-pnt-chain
seg-31c8acef1aed = not exported in this release: the fused positioning kinds place their satellites in an Earth-fixed frame relative to an epoch they never name, so a time-tagged export would have to invent the calendar date (export the <c1/> or <c2/> scenario instead)

# text
seg-3c57b084f0d7 = the kind's geometry is centred on the Moon, Mars or another solar-system body; CZML, KML and GeoJSON describe positions on or about the Earth, and this release writes STK ephemerides with CentralBody Earth only

# text
# c1: constellation-design
seg-2edebe91839c = not exported in this release: <c1/> places its satellites in a body-fixed frame relative to an epoch it never names, so a time-tagged export would have to invent the calendar date

# text
# c1: earth-gnss-lunar
seg-951ac7c5f138 = not exported in this release: the Earth Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) constellation of <c1/> is seen from a receiver at lunar distance, whose trajectory the kind does not expose as a time series

# text
# c1: entry-heating
seg-3cf7f7460c55 = not exported in this release: the <c1/> kind reports peak loads and heating, and keeps no time-tagged ground track

# text
# c1: gnss-ssv
seg-cfc3df59e5c8 = the <c1/> kind sweeps availability and link strength for a user on a shell or a Keplerian trajectory; it has no single position to place on a map

# text
# c1: launch-window
seg-6271953188bd = the <c1/> scenario gives the launch site's latitude but no longitude, so the site cannot be placed

# text
# c1: leo-navmsg
# c2: theta0_deg
seg-c9a63dfb34db = not exported in this release: the <c1/> truth orbit is a fitting reference whose Earth rotation angle at the epoch is an input (<c2/>, default { "0" }), not derived from the calendar date, so its Earth-fixed positions are not tied to a date

